98 508 Q 390 787 390 1178 Q 390 1517 560 1789 Q 730 2061 1120 2304 Q 1197 2349 1200 2377 Q 1203 2406 1126 2445 Q 947 2547 809 2688 Q 672 2829 595 3014 Q 518 3200 518 3437 Q 518 3827 720 4137 Q 922 4448 1286 4630 Q 1651 4813 2131 4813 L 2131 4813 z\nM 2138 4365 Q 1850 4365 1613 4249 Q 1376 4134 1238 3939 Q 1101 3744 1101 3488 Q 1101 2906 1760 2758 L 2419 2605 Q 2509 2579 2633 2649 Q 2758 2720 2880 2854 Q 3002 2989 3078 3161 Q 3155 3334 3155 3514 Q 3155 3891 2880 4128 Q 2605 4365 2138 4365 L 2138 4365 z\nM 2144 384 Q 2675 384 2988 614 Q 3302 845 3302 1235 Q 3302 1530 3142 1715 Q 2982 1901 2624 1984 L 1792 2189 Q 1696 2214 1555 2153 Q 1414 2093 1273 1961 Q 1133 1830 1033 1648 Q 934 1466 934 1248 Q 934 838 1244 611 Q 1555 384 2144 384 L 2144 384 z\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"/></defs><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-88\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-ee\" transform=\"translate(66.796875 0)\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_14\"><g style=\"fill: #333333\" transform=\"translate(206.287531 71.952) scale(0.08 -0.08)\"><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-81\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-81\" transform=\"translate(46.5 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-ee\" transform=\"translate(93 0)\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_15\"><g style=\"fill: #333333\" transform=\"translate(271.054009 46.608) scale(0.08 -0.08)\"><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-81\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-85\" transform=\"translate(46.5 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-ee\" transform=\"translate(111.90625 0)\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_16\"><g style=\"fill: #333333\" transform=\"translate(335.904861 14.928) scale(0.08 -0.08)\"><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-82\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-80\" transform=\"translate(59.90625 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-ee\" transform=\"translate(128.703125 0)\"/></g></g></g></g><defs><clipPath id=\"pccc2e31333\"><rect x=\"40.691281\" y=\"10.56\" width=\"345.96\" height=\"133.056\"/></clipPath></defs></svg>\n","title":"Exhibit 1 · Share of pupils whose families pay for maths tutoring, by household income (%)","note":null},"answer":"<p>Average share paying for maths tutoring = average of the five income groups = (6% + 8% + 11% + 15% + 20%) ÷ 5 = <b>12%</b> (each group is one fifth of pupils).</p> <p>Tutored pupils: 3.6M × 12% = <b>432k</b>.</p> <p>Spend: 432k × 30 sessions × $35 = <b>$454M a year</b>.</p> <p><b>So-what:</b> a market of roughly $450M, heavily skewed to higher-income families, who are the most likely to buy one-to-one tutors.</p>","note":"Pupils in years 7 to 11 in England: about 3.6M. Tutored pupils have about 30 sessions a year at around $35. All figures are case assumptions."},{"q":"How many students does BrightStep need to reach $10M, and what share of tutored pupils is that?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>Revenue per student: 30 lessons × $12 = $360 a year.</p> <p>Students needed: $10M ÷ $360 ≈ <b>27.8k</b>, which is <b>6.4%</b> of today's 432k tutored pupils.</p> <p><b>So-what:</b> ambitious but plausible, and BrightStep's low price could also attract families who don't pay for tutoring today, especially in the lower-income groups where only 6% to 8% currently pay.</p>"},{"q":"What would make you more or less confident in the $10M target?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<ul><li><b>More confident:</b> schools buying lessons for whole groups (one sale, many pupils); strong retention from autumn to exam season; a low cost to win each family</li> <li><b>Less confident:</b> most tutoring is bought just before exams (fewer than 30 sessions); one-to-one tutors cutting prices online; families seeing small groups as \"not real tutoring\"</li> <li><b>Test:</b> retention from September to May and the cost of winning each paying family</li></ul>"}],"reco":"Paid maths tutoring for secondary pupils in England is worth roughly $454M a year: about 432k pupils, 30 sessions each at around $35. BrightStep's $10M target needs about 27.8k students, 6.4% of today's tutored pupils, which is achievable if its $12 price brings in families who can't afford one-to-one tutors. I'd back the plan subject to two checks: retention through the school year, and the cost of acquiring each family. Next step: run a term-long pilot with two schools to measure both.","great":["Uses the income split to get the average, not a guess","Keeps units clear: pupils, sessions, price","Compares the target with the size of the market","Sees the low price could expand the market"],"id":27,"slug":"brightstep-tutoring"}